ence that is so  exciting  talking to Ben Scully the Dodger  announcer as he will wrap up 80 years is  that what it's gonna be then well no not  exactly  I'm wrapping up 67 years doing  play-by-play for the Brooklyn Dodgers  and the LA Dodgers the 80 if you don't  mind me telling you it's kind of an  interesting story do we have time for  this sure oh okay yeah I was about eight  and a half years old  not quite nine and I was coming home  from grammar school walking the 20  blocks or whatever from my school and it  was October the 2nd 1936 and I walked by  a Chinese Laundry and in the window was  the line score of the World Series game  played that day and I stopped and looked  and the Yankees beat the Giants Oh  helped me 18 to 4 doesn't sound like a  World Series game and as a small child  when I looked at it my first thought was  oh those poor Giants so since the Polo  Grounds was only 20 blocks from my  school and the fact that we got out at  2:30 and the games began at 3:15 plus  the fact I was a member of the Police  Athletic League and the Catholic youth  organization  I could go to games free Monday through  Friday so I became a rabid Giants fans  my last game will be October the 2nd  2016 with the giant exactly 80 years to  the day when I first saw that World  Series game and fell in love with  baseball that's awesome  do you have a plan can you plan your  final broadcast I don't want to  I've never planned I really don't want  to make a big deal out of it because  it's already been made a big deal out of  it I'm I'm appreciative but I'm also  embarrassed I've never wanted to get out  in front of the game and for a long time  I was able to keep that game center  stage and I was the voice off in the  wings now that it's my last year  considering all the years that I did  it's become a kind of a big deal and it  is embarrassing most appreciative but  embarrassing so I don't want to break  down on the air I don't want to get  overly emotional and I might so I'm  going to try very hard to be as simple  as possible and let it go at that  the I can tell you this much there was a  there's a letter that I wrote which will  be given out to all the fans I think  starting Friday the 23rd and it's a  letter of dedication to the fans and  then there's something else I did which  will be I think quite a shock quite a  surprise but I'm not saying what that is  but that will occur on my last day at  Dodger Stadium on the 25th of September  then the last game will be in San  Francisco and I would just like to do  that game and that would be that but you  understand your family member I mean  this this is hard for the for the  listeners that mean it I can't imagine  how emotional be for them when they're  listening and then let alone you  trying to wrap up 67 years and say  thanks I mean that gonna be pretty  emotional  pretty emotional game there October 2nd  VIN I I would think so I'm relying on  the Giants and the Dodgers to making it  a good game an exciting game so I can  kind of do what I love to do keep the  game out in front and I'll kind of hide  behind it I hope how tough is it to no  matter what the Dodgers do in the  postseason that you won't be  broadcasting those games well you know  Dan I gave it some thought naturally and  I didn't want to sound like I'm saying  goodbye and Grand Opera where you say  goodbye I'm going I have to go did I  tell you I'm going I didn't want to do  that and I thought you know I'm gonna  say goodbye in Los Angeles at Dodger  Stadium and then I'm going to say  goodbye up in San Francisco for the end  of it I didn't want to do all those and  then come back and do radio in the  postseason and and don't misunderstand  me I'm not putting radio down I just  mean that I will have said goodbye twice  and I think that's enough then if you  could hold on I'd love to have some I  have some more questions for you I got a  lot of questions for you so when we come  back I'm gonna ask you about the moment  